    'Algorithms as a discipline: RAM model, asymptotics, divide-and-conquer, recurrences, heaps, hashing, graph search as an algorithmic tool, sorting lower bounds, greedy, DP, shortest paths, MSTs, and a cartoon-accurate look at NP and reductions. Implementations in Java or Python; proofs on paper. CLRS is the textbook because it is the standard and it is on O’Reilly.',
  whyLanguageOrBook:
    'Language-agnostic algorithms with Java or Python implementations — you already have both. Primary lectures: MIT 6.006. Textbook: CLRS (Cormen et al.) on O’Reilly; it is the reference the field still points at. Tim Roughgarden’s Stanford algorithms lectures (YouTube / Coursera audit) are a second camera. CS 215 built the structures; this course asks whether they are the right ones and why the running time is what you claim.',
  outcomes: [
    'Write recurrences and solve the common ones (Master theorem / recursion trees) without waving hands.',
    'Prove correctness of a greedy or DP with an invariant or substructure argument that a TA could grade.',
    'Implement and empirically compare algorithms; know when the constants eat the asymptotics.',
    'Run Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, Kruskal/Prim, and say which hypotheses each needs.',
    'State P vs NP in a way that is not a TED-talk cartoon, and write one simple reduction idea.',
    'Choose an algorithm for a problem and defend the choice.',
  ],
